00:02:30.850 --> 00:02:35.240
and it's a growing environment.

00:02:35.240 --> 00:02:39.852
So the core of our talk is
high performance computing.

00:02:39.852 --> 00:02:41.310
What is high
performance computing?

00:02:41.310 --> 00:02:43.226
Well, at the Hutch, high
performance computing

00:02:43.226 --> 00:02:46.160
is pretty much computational
resource beyond what's

00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:48.140
available under your desk.

00:02:48.140 --> 00:02:51.610
We scale horizontally, meaning
we use commodity hardware.

00:02:51.610 --> 00:02:55.130
We just add more hardware
as we need more capacity.

00:02:55.130 --> 00:02:57.509
It's what you might call a
Beowulf, or a shared nothing

00:02:57.509 --> 00:02:59.300
style cluster, versus
something like a Blue

00:02:59.300 --> 00:03:02.540
Gene, a Cray, or one of
those more exotic machines.

00:03:02.540 --> 00:03:03.360
Why do we need it?

00:03:03.360 --> 00:03:05.660
Well, we've got about
seven years left

00:03:05.660 --> 00:03:08.850
to cure this cancer problem,
so we need a lot of compute.

00:03:08.850 --> 00:03:11.900
We're answering and
asking a lot of questions

00:03:11.900 --> 00:03:15.101
that require a significant
amount of compute capacity.

00:03:18.400 --> 00:03:22.150
So how do we use
HPC at the Hutch?

00:03:22.150 --> 00:03:26.340
I've just picked out three
research projects here.

00:03:26.340 --> 00:03:28.530
Give you a quick
introduction to what sort

00:03:28.530 --> 00:03:31.020
of work they're doing.

00:03:31.020 --> 00:03:33.270
The Optides Project
is a collaboration

00:03:33.270 --> 00:03:36.990
that identifies new
drugs and therapies

00:03:36.990 --> 00:03:39.750
for very treatment resistant
tumors of the head, neck,

00:03:39.750 --> 00:03:42.960
throat, and of
the brain as well.

00:03:42.960 --> 00:03:46.590
One of their key problems is how
you get drugs across the blood

00:03:46.590 --> 00:03:49.830
brain membrane to
deal with brain

00:03:49.830 --> 00:03:53.280
cancers and other diseases.

00:03:53.280 --> 00:03:54.930
They're a very diverse project.

00:03:54.930 --> 00:03:56.610
They work a lot of
proteomics data.

00:03:56.610 --> 00:04:00.120
They use a lot of genetics
data, high throughput sequencing

00:04:00.120 --> 00:04:02.700
based calls and
alignments, as well as

00:04:02.700 --> 00:04:06.480
protein structure and
functional predictions.

00:04:06.480 --> 00:04:08.770
Just to give you an idea
of the scale of their work,

00:04:08.770 --> 00:04:12.530
they've used 141,000 CPU
hours since the 1st of May.

00:04:15.050 --> 00:04:19.200
The Malik Lab is run
by Dr. Hartmut Malik.

00:04:19.200 --> 00:04:23.250
He's part of our basic
sciences division.

00:04:23.250 --> 00:04:27.090
I've read through his
area of research twice,

00:04:27.090 --> 00:04:29.617
and I still don't
entirely understand it.

00:04:29.617 --> 00:04:31.200
He's basically looking
at the problems

00:04:31.200 --> 00:04:33.150
that can be classified
under genetics

00:04:33.150 --> 00:04:35.280
of evolutionary conflict.

00:04:35.280 --> 00:04:37.480
Does a lot of de
novo genome assembly.

00:04:37.480 --> 00:04:40.052
So new genomes come in, they
try to put them together,

00:04:40.052 --> 00:04:41.760
figure out what's
interesting about that,

00:04:41.760 --> 00:04:43.470
how they've changed.

00:04:43.470 --> 00:04:46.650
Short read mapping, single
nucleotide fault polymorphism

00:04:46.650 --> 00:04:48.520
discovery.

00:04:48.520 --> 00:04:51.240
They're probably on the
smaller scale of compute

00:04:51.240 --> 00:04:57.120
for the center at about 11,000
CPU hours since the 1st of May.

00:04:57.120 --> 00:04:59.450
And finally, the GECCO Project.

00:04:59.450 --> 00:05:01.060
This is a really large project.

00:05:01.060 --> 00:05:03.480
It's been ongoing for a
number of years at the center.

00:05:03.480 --> 00:05:05.820
We've got about 40,000
participants in this one.

00:05:05.820 --> 00:05:09.830
And this one is basically
aimed at colorectal cancers

00:05:09.830 --> 00:05:12.945
and how the environment
affects your risk for getting

00:05:12.945 --> 00:05:15.150
a colorectal cancer.

00:05:15.150 --> 00:05:17.710
They do a lot of work
with whole genome.

00:05:17.710 --> 00:05:20.290
So they do a lot of work
looking for new genotypes

00:05:20.290 --> 00:05:22.200
in the existing data sets.

00:05:22.200 --> 00:05:25.500
G by E, which is genetic
versus the environment, how

00:05:25.500 --> 00:05:27.240
the environment
affects your genome,

00:05:27.240 --> 00:05:32.381
and then how that transfers into
your risk of getting a cancer.

00:05:32.381 --> 00:05:34.380
Like I said, they're one
of the larger projects.

00:05:34.380 --> 00:05:37.460
They've used about 205,000 core
hours since the 1st of May.

00:05:40.710 --> 00:05:45.370
So with an institution
this large,

00:05:45.370 --> 00:05:49.540
we have a very diverse toolset.

00:05:49.540 --> 00:05:50.790
We have a few of the big ones.

00:05:50.790 --> 00:05:54.060
Our bioconductor is our meat and
bread and the first class tools

00:05:54.060 --> 00:05:55.260
that we maintain.

00:05:55.260 --> 00:05:57.450
We're getting more
interest in machine

00:05:57.450 --> 00:06:00.070
learning these days as we
move into more clinical data.

00:06:00.070 --> 00:06:02.790
And then we've got the old
workhorses, SAMtools, Picard,

00:06:02.790 --> 00:06:06.914
things like that that do your
traditional genetic analysis.

00:06:06.914 --> 00:06:08.580
We've also got a lot
of custom pipelines

00:06:08.580 --> 00:06:10.440
that are being built at the
center where people are trying

00:06:10.440 --> 00:06:12.065
to chain together
different tools using

00:06:12.065 --> 00:06:16.050
a variety of systems, scripts,
more modern things, air

00:06:16.050 --> 00:06:19.080
flow, et cetera, to chain
together various tools

00:06:19.080 --> 00:06:21.730
to answer their problems.

00:06:21.730 --> 00:06:23.820
We maintain all of these
using a couple of things,

00:06:23.820 --> 00:06:26.370
easy build and L mod,
that lets us build

00:06:26.370 --> 00:06:29.160
our tools in a
reproducible manner

00:06:29.160 --> 00:06:31.740
as well as deliver them
easily to our researchers.

00:06:36.190 --> 00:06:38.140
So I discussed the
cluster at the beginning

00:06:38.140 --> 00:06:39.640
of the presentation.

00:06:39.640 --> 00:06:41.170
We're a big Slurm shop.

00:06:41.170 --> 00:06:44.960
We've been using it since
the early 2 dot days.

00:06:44.960 --> 00:06:49.120
It's an open source project that
began at the Lawrence Livermore

00:06:49.120 --> 00:06:50.260
labs.

00:06:50.260 --> 00:06:53.050
It's a very high performance
solution that's used on, well,

00:06:53.050 --> 00:06:58.380
as you can see, there are six
of the top 10 supercomputers.

00:06:58.380 --> 00:07:02.099
The emphasis on
performance is, well,

00:07:02.099 --> 00:07:03.640
it's a key part of
how they developed

00:07:03.640 --> 00:07:07.090
and how it's been
very useful for us.

00:07:07.090 --> 00:07:10.390
The big system tuning
guide indicates

00:07:10.390 --> 00:07:14.920
that they had almost 2 million
tasks on 122,000 compute nodes.

00:07:14.920 --> 00:07:17.110
And they started
those in 322 seconds,

00:07:17.110 --> 00:07:18.910
which is fairly impressive.

00:07:18.910 --> 00:07:22.810
A little bit more down to earth,
30,000 tasks on 15,000 Linux

00:07:22.810 --> 00:07:24.814
nodes, and that took 30 seconds.

00:07:24.814 --> 00:07:26.980
So we're looking at a very
high performance solution

00:07:26.980 --> 00:07:29.210
for managing and distributing
workload to a cluster.

00:07:34.850 --> 00:07:37.820
Unfortunately, this
comes with some costs.

00:07:37.820 --> 00:07:41.720
To get this kind of performance,
all the nodes in the cluster

00:07:41.720 --> 00:07:43.555
must share the
same configuration.

00:07:43.555 --> 00:07:45.180
They all have to know
about each other.

00:07:45.180 --> 00:07:47.559
You can't have nodes
just joining randomly

00:07:47.559 --> 00:07:48.350
throughout the day.

00:07:48.350 --> 00:07:52.370
So the cluster has to
have consistent view

00:07:52.370 --> 00:07:55.650
of what the rest of
the cluster looks like.

00:07:55.650 --> 00:07:57.710
So when you add or
remove nodes, this

00:07:57.710 --> 00:08:00.380
is a disruptive task where you
have to reconfigure the cluster

00:08:00.380 --> 00:08:04.700
and tell everybody that
there's a new configuration.

00:08:04.700 --> 00:08:06.200
We really needed
to keep with Slurm.

00:08:06.200 --> 00:08:07.580
Like I said, it's
a key strategy,

00:08:07.580 --> 00:08:09.630
key strategic component
of our environment.

00:08:09.630 --> 00:08:12.254
We don't want our researchers to
have to learn a new submit job

00:08:12.254 --> 00:08:13.820
submission system.

00:08:13.820 --> 00:08:16.940
And we wanted them to be able to
just use their existing script,

00:08:16.940 --> 00:08:19.340
run with the same command,
same options, things like that

00:08:19.340 --> 00:08:22.717
whether they're running on
premises or into the cloud.

00:08:22.717 --> 00:08:24.800
MIKE ENSOR: Probably make
a real quick note there.

00:08:24.800 --> 00:08:30.410
Slurm, the company that
builds and develops Slurm,

00:08:30.410 --> 00:08:32.150
actually did a
partnership with Google,

00:08:32.150 --> 00:08:37.340
and did kind of an alpha
release of a dynamic

00:08:37.340 --> 00:08:40.970
basically build up a cluster,
do your work, and tear it down.

00:08:40.970 --> 00:08:43.100
That still hasn't really
been released yet.

00:08:43.100 --> 00:08:44.672
So we'll cover that
a little bit when

00:08:44.672 --> 00:08:46.422
we get into more the
technical side of it.

00:08:46.422 --> 00:08:49.100
But it is important to note that
the challenges we had really

00:08:49.100 --> 00:08:51.140
were the fact that
the configuration has

00:08:51.140 --> 00:08:53.960
to be known ahead of time once
you build your infrastructure,

00:08:53.960 --> 00:08:55.470
so the cluster can communicate.

00:08:55.470 --> 00:08:58.007
There's no cluster
communication amongst new nodes.

00:08:58.007 --> 00:08:59.090
MICHAEL GUTTERIDGE: Right.

00:08:59.090 --> 00:08:59.790
Yeah.

00:08:59.790 --> 00:09:01.970
There are a number of
ways of doing this.

00:09:01.970 --> 00:09:05.300
And since our approach, we
didn't want to change anything

00:09:05.300 --> 00:09:08.510
for our community, we had to
tie into a lot of resources

00:09:08.510 --> 00:09:09.650
that are back on campus.

00:09:09.650 --> 00:09:12.800
So that's a big, what
you want to call it,

00:09:12.800 --> 00:09:15.620
a big boat anchor on
using the cloud is

00:09:15.620 --> 00:09:20.465
that we have to tie into a
lot of on campus resources.

00:09:20.465 --> 00:09:22.340
I don't know how many
of you have worked with

00:09:22.340 --> 00:09:23.750
or are familiar with Slurm.

00:09:23.750 --> 00:09:25.200
It's pretty straightforward.

00:09:25.200 --> 00:09:26.810
You've got a controller
in the middle.

00:09:26.810 --> 00:09:29.900
You've got your daemons that
run on the individual nodes that

00:09:29.900 --> 00:09:32.042
accept work from the controller.

00:09:32.042 --> 00:09:33.500
And then you have
a database daemon

00:09:33.500 --> 00:09:35.999
that sits in the background and
does all the accounting work

00:09:35.999 --> 00:09:38.360
and keeps records about
what jobs were run

00:09:38.360 --> 00:09:40.621
and what jobs are running.

00:09:40.621 --> 00:09:42.620
There's a few different
commands for interfacing

00:09:42.620 --> 00:09:43.328
with the cluster.

00:09:43.328 --> 00:09:45.740
They're listed up
there. srun, sback,

00:09:45.740 --> 00:09:48.909
scancel are ones that just
allow you to dispatch jobs

00:09:48.909 --> 00:09:51.200
to cluster, cancel them, or
see how they're progressing

00:09:51.200 --> 00:09:54.560
through the session.

00:09:54.560 --> 00:09:55.394
There's two options.

00:09:55.394 --> 00:09:57.434
There's a batch option
and an interactive option.

00:09:57.434 --> 00:09:59.480
We do have a lot of users
that use a lot of cores

00:09:59.480 --> 00:10:01.940
interactively where
they will fire up a job,

00:10:01.940 --> 00:10:04.957
and just from a shell
fire off various r tasks

00:10:04.957 --> 00:10:05.790
or things like that.

00:10:05.790 --> 00:10:11.464
So it's a very, very diverse
workflow throughout the center.

00:10:11.464 --> 00:10:13.130
Data management, here,
where's the data?

00:10:13.130 --> 00:10:14.310
Well, it's all on campus.

00:10:14.310 --> 00:10:16.340
So right now, we come
back to the campus

00:10:16.340 --> 00:10:19.940
from our various cloud clusters
to read and write data.

00:10:19.940 --> 00:10:22.580
That's a bit of another
anchor and another constraint

00:10:22.580 --> 00:10:25.490
on our design as well as
the fact that we're still

00:10:25.490 --> 00:10:27.980
using LDAP and the
Kerberos authentication

00:10:27.980 --> 00:10:29.970
back to on-campus hosts.

00:10:33.580 --> 00:10:34.600
So that's the problem.

00:10:34.600 --> 00:10:37.240
We have a tightly coupled
infrastructure dependent

00:10:37.240 --> 00:10:38.800
computing environment,
and we want

00:10:38.800 --> 00:10:42.910
to take that and
replicate it in the cloud.

00:10:42.910 --> 00:10:46.030
So ongoing, cloud is going to
be a big part of our computing

00:10:46.030 --> 00:10:47.420
strategy.

00:10:47.420 --> 00:10:51.190
We are adopting a cloud
first approach to computing.

00:10:51.190 --> 00:10:54.340
It gives us a lot of options,
allows us to scale capacity

00:10:54.340 --> 00:10:58.420
on demand, and then scale it
back as circumstances warrant.

00:10:58.420 --> 00:11:00.370
We also get new options
and new technologies

00:11:00.370 --> 00:11:03.010
easily and quickly rather
than having to wait

00:11:03.010 --> 00:11:04.670
to provision things on campus.

00:11:10.240 --> 00:11:13.620
We've deployed into
another cloud provider.

00:11:13.620 --> 00:11:16.330
I'll leave it to you
to guess which one.

00:11:16.330 --> 00:11:18.630
And that's been
moderately successful

00:11:18.630 --> 00:11:20.910
given the constraints of
having to use on campus

00:11:20.910 --> 00:11:22.350
resources for data access.

00:11:22.350 --> 00:11:25.861
But for some work, we're
doing a lot of simulation,

00:11:25.861 --> 00:11:27.360
it's been highly
successful in being

00:11:27.360 --> 00:11:28.901
able to give people
the capacity they

00:11:28.901 --> 00:11:31.060
need to accomplish their goals.

00:11:31.060 --> 00:11:35.331
So what we wanted to do
next was expand into GCP.

00:11:35.331 --> 00:11:37.330
There's a lot of exciting
things going on there.

00:11:37.330 --> 00:11:39.330
There's some new capabilities
and different ways

00:11:39.330 --> 00:11:41.660
of managing resources.

00:11:41.660 --> 00:11:46.440
So it's fairly exciting to
you to approach Google and see

00:11:46.440 --> 00:11:47.850
what we can gain from there.

00:11:47.850 --> 00:11:50.370
There's a chance for
us to really diversify

00:11:50.370 --> 00:11:53.370
our infrastructure as well as
mitigate any other risk that

00:11:53.370 --> 00:11:55.230
might come along with
having a single vendor

00:11:55.230 --> 00:11:58.160
solution for our
cloud computing.

00:12:00.750 --> 00:12:03.150
But how do we
solve this problem?

00:12:03.150 --> 00:12:06.210
We didn't really have
any experience with GCP.

00:12:06.210 --> 00:12:08.860
We'd been working with
Amazon for quite some time.

00:12:08.860 --> 00:12:10.710
So we want to make
this happen quickly.

00:12:10.710 --> 00:12:12.240
We want to use best practices.

00:12:12.240 --> 00:12:14.100
We want to have a
solid deployment,

00:12:14.100 --> 00:12:16.330
but we don't really know
what they are for Google.

00:12:16.330 --> 00:12:17.850
So this is where
we looked to bring

00:12:17.850 --> 00:12:20.430
in a partner that had the
experience with Google

00:12:20.430 --> 00:12:22.680
to help us get the solution.

00:12:22.680 --> 00:12:25.950
We have Dev9, a Google
subject matter expert,

00:12:25.950 --> 00:12:29.520
experienced with GCP,
with biology, health care,

00:12:29.520 --> 00:12:31.740
and as well as being
Northwest neighbors.

00:12:31.740 --> 00:12:35.254
So at this point, I'll turn
over the podium to Mike Ensor.

00:12:35.254 --> 00:12:36.170
MIKE ENSOR: All right.

00:12:36.170 --> 00:12:38.760
So as Michael had
mentioned, we're

00:12:38.760 --> 00:12:41.010
a partner here with Google.

00:12:41.010 --> 00:12:42.870
And the nice thing
is I get to mention

00:12:42.870 --> 00:12:44.670
this, every time I go
to downtown Seattle,

00:12:44.670 --> 00:12:45.840
I drive by their campus.

00:12:45.840 --> 00:12:46.790
They have a big sign.

00:12:46.790 --> 00:12:48.474
It's a cool campus.

00:12:48.474 --> 00:12:50.640
So it's nice that they were
kind of in our backyard.

00:12:50.640 --> 00:12:54.420
We do have, our company
does have some health care

00:12:54.420 --> 00:12:58.680
and research background as
well for some of the solutions

00:12:58.680 --> 00:13:01.630
that we've built. So I'll
just go this real fast.

00:13:01.630 --> 00:13:02.490
Who are we?

00:13:02.490 --> 00:13:03.000
We're Dev9.

00:13:03.000 --> 00:13:05.610
We are a custom software
development company based out

00:13:05.610 --> 00:13:07.380
of the Pacific Northwest.

00:13:07.380 --> 00:13:09.870
We introduced ourselves
into the cloud world

00:13:09.870 --> 00:13:12.070
about six or so years ago.

00:13:12.070 --> 00:13:14.960
At that time, we realized
cloud was not a trend,

00:13:14.960 --> 00:13:16.790
and we still stand by that.

00:13:16.790 --> 00:13:19.140
We think it's going to
stick around for a while.

00:13:19.140 --> 00:13:20.945
So that got us into the game.

00:13:20.945 --> 00:13:23.070
We started working with
Google about two years ago,

00:13:23.070 --> 00:13:24.640
and became a premier partner.

00:13:24.640 --> 00:13:27.540
We've got 25 some
certified engineers.

00:13:27.540 --> 00:13:30.160
So when coming here to get
introduced to Fred Hutch,

00:13:30.160 --> 00:13:32.370
it was actually a really,
really awesome time

00:13:32.370 --> 00:13:34.590
to be able to share how
we build infrastructure

00:13:34.590 --> 00:13:36.570
and how we build
outer solutions,

00:13:36.570 --> 00:13:39.820
and we do it slightly different,
at least we think we do.

00:13:39.820 --> 00:13:42.630
And we'll talk about what
that solution is here.

00:13:42.630 --> 00:13:47.420
So recap some of what it is that
we were trying to accomplish,

00:13:47.420 --> 00:13:49.800
what the problem we
were trying to solve.

00:13:49.800 --> 00:13:52.130
We had some unique constraints.

00:13:52.130 --> 00:13:55.590
They have 400 some
projects going on,

00:13:55.590 --> 00:13:59.220
thousands of researchers around
the world who are actually

00:13:59.220 --> 00:14:02.910
using these tools, and we
didn't want to interrupt that.

00:14:02.910 --> 00:14:05.320
We didn't want to change
that world for them.

00:14:05.320 --> 00:14:07.995
What we wanted to give them is
yet just a different option,

00:14:07.995 --> 00:14:10.176
a command line switch
for them to say,

00:14:10.176 --> 00:14:11.550
I want to try a
different cluster

00:14:11.550 --> 00:14:13.072
or work with a
different cluster.

00:14:13.072 --> 00:14:15.030
Some of the unique things
we ended up coming up

00:14:15.030 --> 00:14:16.950
with-- so we get to use
all the [? Skylake, ?]

00:14:16.950 --> 00:14:18.450
so a lot of the
performance actually

00:14:18.450 --> 00:14:22.390
is coming out to
show pretty strong.

00:14:22.390 --> 00:14:24.660
So this is what we
started out with.

00:14:24.660 --> 00:14:27.150
When we jumped in
here, there was already

00:14:27.150 --> 00:14:29.131
an AWS implementation.

00:14:29.131 --> 00:14:30.630
Michael and his
team did a great job

00:14:30.630 --> 00:14:33.700
at putting that together
to show the two sides.

00:14:33.700 --> 00:14:37.441
I originally started seeing the
bottom portion of the diagram

00:14:37.441 --> 00:14:37.940
there.

00:14:37.940 --> 00:14:38.880
That was the original.

00:14:38.880 --> 00:14:40.740
Everything was on premise.

00:14:40.740 --> 00:14:44.790
Everything was set up to be
networks, storage arrays,

00:14:44.790 --> 00:14:45.300
and whatnot.

00:14:45.300 --> 00:14:47.280
That's where all the
data actually lives.

00:14:47.280 --> 00:14:51.540
It was expanded into basically
an almost exact copy into AWS

00:14:51.540 --> 00:14:54.220
and tied together with a VPN.

00:14:54.220 --> 00:15:00.870
Where possible, they dropped
bucket storage for S3

00:15:00.870 --> 00:15:04.840
and other types of
technologies there.

00:15:04.840 --> 00:15:08.760
So before we get into what
we did for our side of it,

00:15:08.760 --> 00:15:11.430
I want to maybe take a small
step back and talk about

00:15:11.430 --> 00:15:15.425
what is multi cloud, and
why their approach, like

00:15:15.425 --> 00:15:16.800
why we agree with
their approach,

00:15:16.800 --> 00:15:20.400
and we consistently
have a lot of clients

00:15:20.400 --> 00:15:24.750
who end up approaching both AWS
and GCP, maybe a little Azure

00:15:24.750 --> 00:15:25.723
here and there.

00:15:25.723 --> 00:15:26.670
Anyway.

00:15:26.670 --> 00:15:28.170
So what does is multi cloud?

00:15:28.170 --> 00:15:29.710
I think you guys have
heard this multiple times

00:15:29.710 --> 00:15:30.418
over the keynote.

00:15:30.418 --> 00:15:32.370
I'm not going to go too
deep into that itself.

00:15:32.370 --> 00:15:34.161
But I think an interesting
note to see here

00:15:34.161 --> 00:15:37.770
is that a research that just
came out, 81% of enterprises

00:15:37.770 --> 00:15:39.690
have a multi cloud strategy.

00:15:39.690 --> 00:15:42.840
A lot of the time, they say that
other cloud is a private cloud,

00:15:42.840 --> 00:15:49.860
but they generally will pick
a private cloud internally

00:15:49.860 --> 00:15:51.464
and then expand it out to one.

00:15:51.464 --> 00:15:53.130
We're starting to see
that trend change,

00:15:53.130 --> 00:15:55.830
and we're starting
to see more cloud

00:15:55.830 --> 00:15:58.435
adoption on multiple cloud,
so it's not just one.

00:15:58.435 --> 00:16:00.810
And that's where we start
building the tools that we need

00:16:00.810 --> 00:16:03.720
in order to be able to
facilitate and interface

00:16:03.720 --> 00:16:06.860
into the different clouds.

00:16:06.860 --> 00:16:08.360
There are some big benefits.

00:16:08.360 --> 00:16:10.180
One, avoiding a vendor lock in.

00:16:10.180 --> 00:16:13.730
In a moment, I'll describe that
as also being a negative too.

00:16:13.730 --> 00:16:15.390
But avoiding a vendor lock in.

00:16:15.390 --> 00:16:18.170
So if you need to move
your applications around

00:16:18.170 --> 00:16:21.390
or your computer
around, it's easy to do.

00:16:21.390 --> 00:16:22.790
It's nice when there's outages.

00:16:22.790 --> 00:16:25.430
If one company's
still able to continue

00:16:25.430 --> 00:16:28.340
to do their work when
somebody fat fingers S3,

00:16:28.340 --> 00:16:29.810
and it goes down.

00:16:29.810 --> 00:16:32.930
It's kind of nice.

00:16:32.930 --> 00:16:37.190
Also with multi cloud, you get
to take the best in breed tools

00:16:37.190 --> 00:16:38.780
for each of the
different vendors.

00:16:38.780 --> 00:16:41.060
I think that's an important
piece to think about.

00:16:41.060 --> 00:16:44.880
Vendor lock in's OK, if you're
using it for the right purpose.

00:16:44.880 --> 00:16:47.540
So if the tool, and you
know, for example here,

00:16:47.540 --> 00:16:50.690
we're going to now enable
the researchers to start

00:16:50.690 --> 00:16:53.660
using things like genomics
API, which they technically

00:16:53.660 --> 00:16:55.629
didn't really have
an easy access into.

00:16:55.629 --> 00:16:57.920
Now that we start having that
relationship with Google,

00:16:57.920 --> 00:16:59.750
that data's closer.

00:16:59.750 --> 00:17:02.430
They can start using the APIs.

00:17:02.430 --> 00:17:05.670
So that's a best in
breed type of tool.

00:17:05.670 --> 00:17:07.940
So there are challenges
to doing multi cloud.

00:17:07.940 --> 00:17:10.960
And I think this is kind of
a cool little Venn diagram I

00:17:10.960 --> 00:17:11.720
drew up here.

00:17:11.720 --> 00:17:15.319
Where if when you get into
it, if you really only want

00:17:15.319 --> 00:17:17.520
to be completely
agnostic, you end up

00:17:17.520 --> 00:17:19.176
with the lowest
common denominator,

00:17:19.176 --> 00:17:20.800
meaning that you're
only doing compute.

00:17:20.800 --> 00:17:23.170
You're only doing network.

00:17:23.170 --> 00:17:24.710
Well, that's about it actually.

00:17:24.710 --> 00:17:27.380
So if you want to use any of the
cool features and functionality

00:17:27.380 --> 00:17:30.532
that come as a managed provider,
there's none available.

00:17:30.532 --> 00:17:32.240
So keep that in mind
when you're thinking

00:17:32.240 --> 00:17:37.520
about where your strategies
are with doing multi cloud.

00:17:37.520 --> 00:17:40.832
It also does increase
maintenance and governance.

00:17:40.832 --> 00:17:43.040
So you have to maintain
multiple different platforms.

00:17:43.040 --> 00:17:45.590
And that means it's
also operationally

00:17:45.590 --> 00:17:49.610
now you have folks, teams
who have to understand

00:17:49.610 --> 00:17:50.930
all the platforms.

00:17:50.930 --> 00:17:54.750
That can be a challenge to
a lot of companies as well.

00:17:54.750 --> 00:17:58.410
So how do we go
about doing this?

00:17:58.410 --> 00:18:00.410
As an industry,
we've built the tools

00:18:00.410 --> 00:18:02.750
now to allow us to
have a little bit

00:18:02.750 --> 00:18:05.630
of a homogeneous infrastructure.

00:18:05.630 --> 00:18:10.100
So we heavily use, we're very
are big fans of HashiCorp

00:18:10.100 --> 00:18:12.380
in general as a company.

00:18:12.380 --> 00:18:14.379
HashiCorp has a
product called Packer.

00:18:14.379 --> 00:18:15.920
What it allows you
to do is it allows

00:18:15.920 --> 00:18:21.300
you to define what you want
an image or a virtual machine

00:18:21.300 --> 00:18:23.540
to look like, and it will
build that image for you

00:18:23.540 --> 00:18:26.280
and either push it into
the cloud you want to,

00:18:26.280 --> 00:18:28.190
or build it within
the cloud you want to.

00:18:28.190 --> 00:18:31.430
So with that, we can have
the same base level of an AWS

00:18:31.430 --> 00:18:33.140
image, we can have
the same one in GCP,

00:18:33.140 --> 00:18:35.297
and the same one
on premise, largely

00:18:35.297 --> 00:18:37.130
with just the same file,
and you just run it

00:18:37.130 --> 00:18:38.510
with different providers.

00:18:38.510 --> 00:18:43.190
So that sets us up to create
a base level at that point.

00:18:43.190 --> 00:18:46.940
Then on top of that,
we look at what

00:18:46.940 --> 00:18:51.410
other binaries and applications
need to be on those machines.

00:18:51.410 --> 00:18:54.262
One of those, in this case
here, a lot of the Slurm,

00:18:54.262 --> 00:18:55.970
in the cluster a lot
of the Slurm daemons

00:18:55.970 --> 00:18:58.040
and those types of
things, applications

00:18:58.040 --> 00:18:59.970
have to be on each of
the different machines.

00:18:59.970 --> 00:19:01.930
So in that case, we
actually use Chef.

00:19:01.930 --> 00:19:05.990
Chef was a great tool for
configuration management.

00:19:05.990 --> 00:19:08.720
It allowed us to
write once and use

00:19:08.720 --> 00:19:11.930
in multiple different
environments.

00:19:11.930 --> 00:19:17.780
We as a company, we expanded
this into with Fred Hutch here,

00:19:17.780 --> 00:19:21.170
but as a company, we embrace
infrastructure as code.

00:19:21.170 --> 00:19:24.500
It's something that we think
that every company needs to do,

00:19:24.500 --> 00:19:26.720
and we basically
automate everything

00:19:26.720 --> 00:19:28.100
is kind of my little tagline.

00:19:28.100 --> 00:19:33.230
I talk about that way too
much, but it needs to be said.

00:19:33.230 --> 00:19:35.810
So when we came in for
Google, and this is very

00:19:35.810 --> 00:19:38.296
specific to Google, we wrote--

00:19:38.296 --> 00:19:40.670
I'm sure all of you guys, if
you've done stuff on Google,

00:19:40.670 --> 00:19:42.230
you immediately know that
it's not the easiest thing

00:19:42.230 --> 00:19:43.354
in the world to start with.

00:19:43.354 --> 00:19:44.600
You get a whole pile of docs.

00:19:44.600 --> 00:19:46.340
You say, now what?

00:19:46.340 --> 00:19:48.530
What we did is we wrote
a foundation layer.

00:19:48.530 --> 00:19:50.180
We used Terraform.

00:19:50.180 --> 00:19:52.940
So what this does, it
allows us to spell out

00:19:52.940 --> 00:19:57.140
what a base network and
project structure looks

00:19:57.140 --> 00:19:59.690
like within a Google project.

00:19:59.690 --> 00:20:02.090
So we can run this.

00:20:02.090 --> 00:20:03.430
We set it up real simply.

00:20:03.430 --> 00:20:06.772
And it defines that, so we
can move products around,

00:20:06.772 --> 00:20:08.480
should we need to, in
a folder structure.

00:20:08.480 --> 00:20:10.670
It also sets up
identity management

00:20:10.670 --> 00:20:14.090
into a layered structure, so
we can do organization folders

00:20:14.090 --> 00:20:16.760
and projects in a hierarchy.

00:20:16.760 --> 00:20:20.645
So that foundation gets
run once, sets it up.

00:20:20.645 --> 00:20:22.520
Then we have what we
call our services layer.

00:20:22.520 --> 00:20:25.400
And this is where we
actually provision, manage

00:20:25.400 --> 00:20:29.870
services, such
things as cloud SQL

00:20:29.870 --> 00:20:32.940
or other dependent
applications that

00:20:32.940 --> 00:20:35.150
are other dependent
managed services that need

00:20:35.150 --> 00:20:38.720
to exist within the project.

00:20:38.720 --> 00:20:41.030
And then at the last layer,
we actually have an active.

00:20:41.030 --> 00:20:42.206
We call it the app layer.

00:20:42.206 --> 00:20:43.580
And this is where
you're actively

00:20:43.580 --> 00:20:46.910
installing or updating or
changing, deploying, rapidly

00:20:46.910 --> 00:20:50.880
changing services within
your infrastructure.

00:20:50.880 --> 00:20:52.440
I don't think I had
on the next one.

00:20:52.440 --> 00:20:53.330
Nope.

00:20:53.330 --> 00:20:54.260
I'll say it anyway.

00:20:54.260 --> 00:20:56.810
So one little tip that
we like to try and say it

00:20:56.810 --> 00:20:59.180
all over talks is when
you're using infrastructure

00:20:59.180 --> 00:21:00.620
as code, if there's
a possibility

00:21:00.620 --> 00:21:03.140
to run it in a continuous
integration environment,

00:21:03.140 --> 00:21:04.850
definitely do that.

00:21:04.850 --> 00:21:08.106
Terraform is another
HashiCorp product.

00:21:08.106 --> 00:21:09.480
What it does, it
allows you to be

00:21:09.480 --> 00:21:11.604
declarative about what you
want your infrastructure

00:21:11.604 --> 00:21:12.880
to look like.

00:21:12.880 --> 00:21:17.160
So you can say, this is
the type of machine I want.

00:21:17.160 --> 00:21:18.960
I want 200 of them.

00:21:18.960 --> 00:21:20.080
And it's just a variable.

00:21:20.080 --> 00:21:21.413
You change that, and you run it.

00:21:21.413 --> 00:21:26.200
And it allows you to give a
declarative format for what

00:21:26.200 --> 00:21:27.480
your infrastructure is.

00:21:27.480 --> 00:21:31.345
When you run that with a CI,
it means that you no longer

00:21:31.345 --> 00:21:33.720
have an individual who has to
run it off a laptop or even

00:21:33.720 --> 00:21:34.540
on a jump box.

00:21:34.540 --> 00:21:37.390
You now can have the same
run happen every single time.

00:21:37.390 --> 00:21:40.800
It's all the benefit you
get from running a CICD.

00:21:40.800 --> 00:21:42.570
A little side effect
of that as well,

00:21:42.570 --> 00:21:45.390
Terraform when it builds
your infrastructure,

00:21:45.390 --> 00:21:47.760
it creates a state file.

00:21:47.760 --> 00:21:49.980
So it knows what
your infrastructure

00:21:49.980 --> 00:21:52.740
looks like without having to go
necessarily back to the cloud.

00:21:52.740 --> 00:21:54.790
So it can manage itself.

00:21:54.790 --> 00:21:56.400
Now along with that,
the benefits are,

00:21:56.400 --> 00:21:59.010
if you want to
heavily govern or you

00:21:59.010 --> 00:22:00.665
want to govern
your infrastructure

00:22:00.665 --> 00:22:03.360
so you're not running
high costs or you

00:22:03.360 --> 00:22:05.700
want to make sure
that individuals only

00:22:05.700 --> 00:22:07.260
have certain access,
you generally

00:22:07.260 --> 00:22:09.360
don't want to give
access to the console

00:22:09.360 --> 00:22:12.340
or to G cloud, which is
the command line tool,

00:22:12.340 --> 00:22:14.330
so use Terraform to
make all your changes.

00:22:14.330 --> 00:22:17.800
It forces you to run through a
pattern when you're using a CI.

00:22:17.800 --> 00:22:18.850
It runs your pattern.

00:22:18.850 --> 00:22:20.850
That you actually get to
do all of your own code

00:22:20.850 --> 00:22:22.600
reviews on your
infrastructure going out.

00:22:22.600 --> 00:22:25.380
So you know exactly what your
infrastructure is at any given

00:22:25.380 --> 00:22:31.840
time, and you can say, a change
doesn't go through or whatnot.

00:22:31.840 --> 00:22:34.910
So talked about our base images.

00:22:34.910 --> 00:22:38.550
So in our case here, our
base images, everything

00:22:38.550 --> 00:22:40.266
is using Ubuntu.

00:22:40.266 --> 00:22:42.740
You guys use some [INAUDIBLE]
No, it's mostly just Ubuntu.

00:22:42.740 --> 00:22:44.240
MICHAEL GUTTERIDGE:
It's all Ubuntu.

00:22:44.240 --> 00:22:45.090
MIKE ENSOR: Yeah.

00:22:45.090 --> 00:22:48.820
So the general idea here is
that you create an image,

00:22:48.820 --> 00:22:50.350
and that image is
being pushed up.

00:22:50.350 --> 00:22:51.600
I already talked through that.

00:22:51.600 --> 00:22:55.020
So here's what the provisioning
looked like for us.

00:22:55.020 --> 00:22:57.510
We started out with
a base OS image,

00:22:57.510 --> 00:23:01.804
and we used just
really nothing on it.

00:23:01.804 --> 00:23:02.970
It was really vanilla.

00:23:02.970 --> 00:23:05.760
On top of that, depending on the
different machines themselves,

00:23:05.760 --> 00:23:09.630
a lot of them needed to have the
tools that the researchers were

00:23:09.630 --> 00:23:10.270
using.

00:23:10.270 --> 00:23:14.960
So for example, the SDK for
R, the SAMtool, TensorFlow, et

00:23:14.960 --> 00:23:15.750
cetera.

00:23:15.750 --> 00:23:17.250
By the way, TensorFlow
is something

00:23:17.250 --> 00:23:19.420
that we are currently
looking into just saying,

00:23:19.420 --> 00:23:22.410
can we add that
on as being early

00:23:22.410 --> 00:23:24.690
add on GPUs into these clusters?

00:23:24.690 --> 00:23:29.424
We haven't done that yet,
but it's on the roadmap.

00:23:29.424 --> 00:23:30.840
And then at the
very top, you know

00:23:30.840 --> 00:23:32.506
if you have all those
Slurm dependencies

00:23:32.506 --> 00:23:33.759
and then working with it.

00:23:33.759 --> 00:23:36.300
Another thing that was kind of
a challenge that we had to fit

00:23:36.300 --> 00:23:38.730
is all of the data, we
said it multiple times,

00:23:38.730 --> 00:23:41.850
but all the data
actually was back at HQ.

00:23:41.850 --> 00:23:46.410
So researchers throw all their
data, everything comes in,

00:23:46.410 --> 00:23:50.540
sits on net storage, and we
use NFS to transfer it out

00:23:50.540 --> 00:23:54.550
to the cloud for the
actual compute itself.

00:23:54.550 --> 00:23:56.520
So there are some
challenges in that solution

00:23:56.520 --> 00:23:59.386
as well just due to latency
that happens with moving

00:23:59.386 --> 00:24:00.510
very large amounts of data.

00:24:04.206 --> 00:24:04.800
All right.

00:24:04.800 --> 00:24:05.720
Actually, I already
covered this,

00:24:05.720 --> 00:24:07.070
so I'm going to skip that slide.

00:24:07.070 --> 00:24:07.569
All right.

00:24:07.569 --> 00:24:12.110
So largely the solution itself
from a technology perspective

00:24:12.110 --> 00:24:15.680
wasn't super difficult,
but what it did

00:24:15.680 --> 00:24:18.920
is it created a homogeneous
infrastructure between all

00:24:18.920 --> 00:24:21.350
of the different providers
that they were working with,

00:24:21.350 --> 00:24:25.780
with on-prem with
VMware, AWS, and GCP.

00:24:25.780 --> 00:24:27.686
So with that, that
allows operations.

00:24:27.686 --> 00:24:29.060
You know I'd
mentioned previously

00:24:29.060 --> 00:24:31.550
that operations when you're
doing multiple different clouds

00:24:31.550 --> 00:24:35.150
can be a nightmare if you
don't have some level of common

00:24:35.150 --> 00:24:37.910
between all of them, so
this gives us that ability.

00:24:37.910 --> 00:24:39.800
So here's kind of
the final deck.

00:24:39.800 --> 00:24:42.180
I kind of grayed
out the AWS one,

00:24:42.180 --> 00:24:44.640
because it's less important now.

00:24:44.640 --> 00:24:45.320
But yeah.

00:24:45.320 --> 00:24:47.840
We actually run this
cluster over a couple

00:24:47.840 --> 00:24:49.550
of different zones,
which is nice.

00:24:49.550 --> 00:24:51.110
And we do have some
high availability

00:24:51.110 --> 00:24:54.020
built into the clustering of
the application-- or, I'm sorry,

00:24:54.020 --> 00:24:56.070
with the Slurm cluster.

00:24:56.070 --> 00:24:58.730
And along with that, we have
a lot of attached storage

00:24:58.730 --> 00:25:00.500
that ends up working with that.

00:25:00.500 --> 00:25:02.450
All the different
systems run over VPN

00:25:02.450 --> 00:25:04.350
back into the general networks.

00:25:04.350 --> 00:25:08.600
So that way, you have
accessibility to the data

00:25:08.600 --> 00:25:11.680
back into HQ.

00:25:11.680 --> 00:25:14.400
So ongoing challenges,
we kind of alluded

00:25:14.400 --> 00:25:17.580
to the fact that Slurm
itself as a product doesn't

00:25:17.580 --> 00:25:22.680
have dynamic clustering or
does not have dynamic scale.

00:25:22.680 --> 00:25:25.800
You can't just throw a new
machine into the cluster

00:25:25.800 --> 00:25:29.190
and have it auto recognized.

00:25:29.190 --> 00:25:33.390
Google did do a product, like I
said, that does help with that,

00:25:33.390 --> 00:25:36.490
but it's not public yet.

00:25:36.490 --> 00:25:40.410
Or the press release
is, but the code isn't.

00:25:40.410 --> 00:25:43.050
So another challenge is, you
know identity management is

00:25:43.050 --> 00:25:45.180
another one too that
we are still making

00:25:45.180 --> 00:25:47.010
sure we try to stay on top of.

00:25:47.010 --> 00:25:49.470
Identity management,
like IM in general, so

00:25:49.470 --> 00:25:52.350
what boxes have access to
what data, what researchers

00:25:52.350 --> 00:25:53.400
have access to what.

00:25:53.400 --> 00:25:57.150
Right now, we have a very, very
generic policy that is maybe

00:25:57.150 --> 00:25:59.136
a little too permissive.

00:25:59.136 --> 00:26:00.510
But it's a challenge,
because you

00:26:00.510 --> 00:26:02.520
have to match that up
with AWS and also what

00:26:02.520 --> 00:26:03.811
you're doing with your on prem.

00:26:07.300 --> 00:26:09.210
All right.

00:26:09.210 --> 00:26:10.890
So at the very
end, so what did we

00:26:10.890 --> 00:26:12.720
do by going through
all of this one?

00:26:12.720 --> 00:26:15.240
We took the existing solution
that Michael and his team

00:26:15.240 --> 00:26:17.484
put together, I would
say we strengthened it

00:26:17.484 --> 00:26:18.150
in certain ways.

00:26:18.150 --> 00:26:20.379
We brought in a lot of
Dev9's best practices

00:26:20.379 --> 00:26:21.920
on how we put together
infrastructure

00:26:21.920 --> 00:26:24.490
as code, how we run it.

00:26:24.490 --> 00:26:26.790
So we strengthened
that, so we did help out

00:26:26.790 --> 00:26:31.410
the AWS and also the on
premise version of the cluster.

00:26:31.410 --> 00:26:34.950
But by virtue, we also added
the capabilities for Google.

00:26:34.950 --> 00:26:38.430
We were able to now provide
a 50/50 split for Google

00:26:38.430 --> 00:26:41.430
and AWS traffic for cloud,
which now means that they

00:26:41.430 --> 00:26:43.730
can scale even larger.

00:26:43.730 --> 00:26:46.140
The amount of data, as
you saw, 1.2 petabytes

00:26:46.140 --> 00:26:48.730
of data, that's a lot of data.

00:26:48.730 --> 00:26:51.570
So having that, they now have
the capacity and the capability

00:26:51.570 --> 00:26:53.520
of growing that even
larger as they need

00:26:53.520 --> 00:26:56.340
and matching the same
amount of compute

00:26:56.340 --> 00:26:58.110
that they need with
the demand they have.

00:26:58.110 --> 00:27:00.210
They don't have a lot of
resource contention now.

00:27:03.250 --> 00:27:05.340
All right, so in
the future, we are

00:27:05.340 --> 00:27:08.490
looking at adding in the
capabilities of using genomics

00:27:08.490 --> 00:27:12.400
API along with any of the
other machine learning.

00:27:12.400 --> 00:27:14.940
The hosted TensorFlow, right
now, a lot of the researchers

00:27:14.940 --> 00:27:17.730
are using TensorFlow,
but its the self managed,

00:27:17.730 --> 00:27:22.350
and it's non-GPU based,
obviously, no TPUs.

00:27:22.350 --> 00:27:25.620
So it's still very
early days for that,

00:27:25.620 --> 00:27:28.290
but a lot of the
data they're doing

00:27:28.290 --> 00:27:30.000
doesn't necessarily
require it, but it's

00:27:30.000 --> 00:27:32.333
something where they want to
start being able to use it.

00:27:32.333 --> 00:27:34.580
So we're expanding
the solution to be

00:27:34.580 --> 00:27:38.220
able to add those types
of capabilities to it.

00:27:38.220 --> 00:27:41.880
Also right now, all the data,
as we mentioned, is on premise,

00:27:41.880 --> 00:27:44.820
but also all the compute
is generally in the US.

00:27:44.820 --> 00:27:48.270
Fred Hutch has multiple
centers around the world.

00:27:48.270 --> 00:27:50.100
And so being able
to actually use

00:27:50.100 --> 00:27:51.780
this same solution,
because we've now

00:27:51.780 --> 00:27:53.550
created this
infrastructure as code,

00:27:53.550 --> 00:27:55.770
we can now create that
same infrastructure

00:27:55.770 --> 00:27:58.440
in multiple different
regions around the world

00:27:58.440 --> 00:28:01.810
and still have the same
infrastructure for them.

00:28:01.810 --> 00:28:03.747
And then the last
thing is, it's a hope,

00:28:03.747 --> 00:28:05.580
but we would like to
actually be able to add

00:28:05.580 --> 00:28:08.670
in some preemptive
machines as well to this

00:28:08.670 --> 00:28:09.914
to help lower the cost.

00:28:09.914 --> 00:28:12.330
And a lot of that's going to
come with the synchronization

00:28:12.330 --> 00:28:15.750
and the automation that we're
doing for identifying when you

00:28:15.750 --> 00:28:18.090
want to build new cluster
or add more to a cluster

00:28:18.090 --> 00:28:19.710
and redeploy it.

00:28:19.710 --> 00:28:22.090
So we're working on
some of those pieces.

00:28:22.090 --> 00:28:24.600
But that will be kind of
a fun next step for us.

00:28:24.600 --> 00:28:28.550
[MUSIC PLAYING]

